    "product_id": "1cf62a0c-c949-4fe8-b249-f24e7cbbef8e",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 110430\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n930 PM PDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* An incoming system will bring widespread precipitation chances and \n  an elevated flash flood risk to the area through the weekend. \n\n* This system will also bring gusty winds to the region over the \n  weekend into early next week.\n\n* Cooler than normal temperatures will stick around through much of \n  next week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through next Saturday.\n\nSunday looks to be the highest impact day, with showers and \nthunderstorms becoming more widespread overnight and continuing \nthroughout the day on Sunday. Flash Flood Watches remain in effect \nfor most of the area through 5:00 AM PDT/MST on Monday. Evening \nsatellite imagery shows post-tropical cyclone Rachel drifting inland \nnear Ensenada on the Baja Peninsula. Rachel's remnant circulation \nwill continue to track northeast, moving into southern San \nBernardino County early Sunday morning, moving up the Colorado River \nValley throughout the morning and early afternoon before moving into \nsouthwestern Utah by late afternoon. As Rachel swings through, \nshowers and thunderstorms will become more widespread through the \nmorning and into the afternoon. Heavy rain, flash flooding, and \nstrong winds will be the primary threats with these storms. High \nresolution models show the potential for a swath of 1.0+ inch rain \ntotals extending across the higher terrain of San Bernardino, into \nDeath Valley, and southern Nevada by late morning. In addition to \nheavy rains, flash flooding, and strong winds, the Storm Prediction \nCenter (SPC) has highlighted the lower Colorado River Valley with a \n2% tornado risk. Should we see a tornado develop, it will be short \nlived and on the lower end of the EF-scale. As the remnants of \nRachel begin to exit our forecast area, w will see an upper-level \ntrough push into the area late Sunday morning/early Sunday \nafternoon. This trough combined with the lingering moisture from \nRachel, will help to fuel another round of showers and thunderstorms \nacross the region. This trough will remain over the area as we go \ninto next week, with chances for isolated-to-scattered convection \nlingering through Tuesday before things dry out Wednesday onward.\n\nAs Rachel's remnant circulation approaches and moves through the \narea, we will see strong southerly winds pick up across the Mojave \nDesert with wind gusts in the 40 to 55 mph range. A Wind Advisory \nwill go into effect at 2:00 AM on Sunday for San Bernardino, Clark, \nand Mohave Counties as Rachel begins to approach our forecast area. \nThis Wind Advisory and the Wind Advisory for the southern Great \nBasin and Death Valley will remain in effect through 5:00 am Monday \nmorning. As Rachel moves into up the Colorado River Valley, we \nexpect to see stronger winds mix down to the surface, with 60+ mph \nwind gusts possible in the Colorado River Valley, Mohave County, \nsouthern and Northeastern Clark County, and Lincoln County. As \nsuch, a High Wind Warning will go into effect for the \naforementioned areas beginning at 5:00 am Sunday morning and \nrunning through 8:00 pm Sunday evening. Breezy winds will linger \ninto early next week, but will not be as intense as winds on \nSunday.\n\nThe aforementioned trough, which will fuel additional storms on \nSunday, will linger over the area through Tuesday, before exiting \nthe region on Wednesday and Thursday. As a result of these low 500 \nmb heights, temperatures will cool each day through Tuesday, \ngradually warming back up during the second half of the week as 500 \nmb heights recover. Even with this late week warm up, temperatures \nwill remain cooler than normal as we head into next weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast \nPackage...Continued deteriorating conditions expected as scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms as well as gusty winds are expected at \ntimes. Low confidence in precipitation chances as scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms are possible through Sunday afternoon, \nthough tried to limit thunderstorm chances to Sunday when there is\na higher chance. Expecting a few bands of rain to move through \ntonight, likely after 08Z. Potential precipitation impacts include\nwhich could bring heavy rain and CIGs to 8000ft. More organized \nshowers and any thunderstorms could contain visibility \nrestrictions due to heavy rain, sudden gusty winds, and CIGs to \n5000ft. Increasing confidence that precipitation the terminals \nwill exit to the northeast by 03Z Sunday evening. \n\nA period of southeast to east winds at or below 8KT are expected \ntonight before strong southeast winds arrive expected Sunday \nmorning, becoming south in the afternoon. Gusts over 30KT are likely \nand gusts over 35KT are possible. Winds become south and diminish \naround sunset on Sunday as gusts drop closer to 25KT for the nigh\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Lighter winds are \nexpected for a period overnight before gusty south to southeast \nwinds develop Sunday morning then continue through the afternoon. \nWidespread gusts over 25-35KT are expected, with gusts over 45KT \npossible in the Colorado River Valley and western Arizona. \n\nShower and thunderstorm activity will continue to shift south to \nnorth overnight, becoming more widespread into Sunday morning. \nhighest chances and coverage of precipitation across California into \nthe southern Great Basin but chances exists across the entire \nregion. Showers chances continue through Sunday, with thunderstorms \npotentially developing in areas of southern Nevada and western \nArizona Sunday afternoon. Potential impacts through the period \ninclude CIGs below 5000ft, lighting, heavy rain with visibility \nrestrictions, and sudden gusty winds. Precipitation will end from \nsouthwest to northeast as it lifts into southwest Utah Sunday \nevening and early overnight. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Stessman\nAVIATION...Nickerson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
